<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otto von Bismarck</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a>–<a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Bismarck</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otto von Bismarck was a Prussian statesman who unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and served as the first chancellor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>. He built a federal constitution that preserved Prussian dominance among the empire's constituent states. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulturkampf">Kulturkampf</a>, opposed socialism, and introduced the world's first national <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-insurance">social insurance</a> system in the 1880s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck.jpg" alt="Otto von Bismarck" width="311" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otto_von_Bismarck_1885_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Otto von Bismarck was born on 1 April 1815 at Schönhausen, a noble estate west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in Prussian Saxony. His father was a Junker estate owner and former Prussian officer, and his mother came from a family of senior civil servants in Berlin. In 1816 the family moved to its Pomeranian estate at Kniephof. He studied law at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-gottingen">University of Göttingen</a> from 1832 to 1833 and then at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humboldt-university-of-berlin">University of Berlin</a>, and he knew English, French, Italian, Polish and Russian.</p><p>Others took him for a typical backwoods Prussian Junker, an image he encouraged by wearing military uniforms. In 1847 he was chosen as a representative to the newly created Prussian legislature, the Vereinigter Landtag, where he gained a reputation as a royalist and reactionary with a gift for stinging rhetoric.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1861 Wilhelm became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and soon clashed with the Landtag, which in 1862 refused funds for a reorganisation of the army. On the advice of Albrecht von Roon he appointed Bismarck Minister President and Foreign Minister on 23 September 1862. Bismarck argued that the constitution had a legal loophole where a budget failed to pass, and kept collecting taxes on the basis of the 1861 budget for four years. With Roon's reorganisation of the army and Moltke's military strategy, his diplomacy brought wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the creation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>.</p><p>In 1871 he was made a prince and the first Imperial Chancellor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, while keeping his Prussian offices, so that he had nearly complete control of domestic and foreign policy. That year he began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulturkampf">Kulturkampf</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, partly from fear of papal power; it failed, and the Catholics formed the Centre Party. His diplomacy aimed to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s position in a peaceful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a> died in 1888 and Frederick III after 99 days. The new emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a>, wanted rapid expansion and a larger German place in the sun. The final split came over Bismarck's attempt to make the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-socialist-laws">Anti-Socialist Laws</a> permanent. Wilhelm refused to open his reign with a bloody campaign against his own subjects, and Bismarck was forced to resign. Historians have praised him as the statesman who united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and have also criticised his authoritarian rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Otto von Bismarck” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Germany has the world's oldest universal health care system, dating from <b>Bismarck</b>'s social legislation of the 1880s; the Federal Republic still insures its population by statute, and spent 12.27% of its GDP on health in 2024.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Prussia, represented at the restored Diet by <b>Otto von Bismarck</b> from 1851, then contested Austria’s leadership at every turn, while the Zollverein bound the smaller states’ economies to Berlin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 3 March 1885 the German government announced an imperial charter, signed by Chancellor <b>Otto von Bismarck</b> on 27 February, which granted Peters' company a protectorate in the African Great Lakes region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire's founder was <b>Otto von Bismarck</b>, whose skill lay in construction more than conquest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the constitutional conflict over army reform, <b>Otto von Bismarck</b>, minister-president from 1862, governed for four years without an approved budget.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the German Empire of 1871, however, <b>Otto von Bismarck</b> rejected the British model: the chancellor answered to the Kaiser, not to the Reichstag.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Schönhausen (Elbe), in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Friedrichsruh, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.58,12.04&amp;year=1868" aria-label="Schönhausen (Elbe) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4820 350 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Otto von Bismarck"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5270 620h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5270 620h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.58,12.04&amp;year=1868">What was here in 1868? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The great questions of the day were not to be decided by speeches and majorities—this had been the error of 1848 and 1849—but by iron and blood!&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to the budget committee of the Prussian diet, 1862 &#183; tr. Kenneth R. H. Mackenzie, 1870 &#183; as reported in George Hesekiel's Life of Bismarck &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/58776/pg58776-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I look on our task as a more useful though a humbler one; it is enough if we can be an honest broker.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to the Reichstag, 1878 &#183; tr. J. W. Headlam, 1899 &#183; as quoted in J. W. Headlam's Bismarck and the Foundation of the German Empire &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12400/pg12400-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We Germans fear God, and naught else in the world! It is this fear of God which makes us love and cherish peace.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to the Reichstag, 6 February 1888 &#183; tr. Edmund von Mach, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/13056/pg13056-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Depend upon it, in nominating a cardinal as our representative at Rome, we neither hoped to talk the pope over to our way of thinking, nor did we at all wish to signify our willingness to repeat a certain ceremony enacted centuries ago at Canossa.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to the Reichstag, 14 May 1872 &#183; tr. in The World's Famous Orations, ed. William Jennings Bryan, 1906 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/The_Canossa_Speech" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We Germans had no longer any reason for war. We had what we needed. To fight for more, from a lust of conquest and for the annexation of countries which were not necessary for us, always appeared to me like an atrocity…&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to students on his eightieth birthday, Friedrichsruh, 1 April 1895 &#183; tr. Edmund von Mach, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/13056/pg13056-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
